>
  <h2>The situation</h2>
  <p>Like most trades businesses, this one ran through the owner. Quotes, orders and questions all came to his phone, and the website did little more than list a number to call. He wanted a website that works like a good counter clerk: customers can figure out what they need, get a price and order, with AI doing real work behind it.</p>

  <h2>What I'm doing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Brand.</strong> A new identity built for the trade, including a stencil wordmark whose letterforms are drawn to be cut from steel with a torch.</li>
    <li><strong>Storefront.</strong> A custom online store that opens with the wordmark being cut from a steel sheet, then gets out of the way so customers can shop.</li>
    <li><strong>Ordering in shop language.</strong> A phone-first flow where a customer picks the metal, the shape and the size, then types a cut list the way they'd say it at the counter, with live totals.</li>
    <li><strong>One lead pipeline.</strong> Service and commercial estimate requests go into a single queue, so nothing lives only in a text message.</li>
    <li><strong>A catalog AI can read.</strong> The product and pricing rules sit in plain, tested files, so the same logic can serve the website, AI shopping assistants and future tools for contractors.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to watch for</h2>
  <p>The measure of success is simple: jobs quoted and orders placed without the owner picking up the phone. I'll add the numbers here once the storefront has been live long enough to have them.</p>

  <h2>Why it matters for your business</h2>
  <p>The AI part only works because the business rules were written down first. Pricing, what the shop will and won't take on, and how a cut list gets checked all became files every tool reads from. That's the same foundation the <a href="/kit/">free AI Foundation Kit</a> helps you build.</p>
</CaseStudy>
